\DOC X86_VERBOSE_STEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_VERBOSE_STEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes one x86 instruction from the current symbolic state,
replacing the state term in an {eventually} goal by a fresh successor state and
adding assumptions describing that state.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {(exth1,exth2) : thm * (thm option array)} is the execution
context for the code being stepped: {exth1} gives the length of the
machine-code list and {exth2} is an array of per-instruction decoding theorems.
The second argument {sname : string} names the new state variable introduced by
the step. The goal must have the form {A ?- eventually x86 P t}, with {t} the
current state variable, and one subgoal results whose conclusion is the same
{eventually} statement at a new state {u} named by {sname}:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 P t
   ========================================  X86_VERBOSE_STEP_TAC (exth1,exth2) sname
   A' ?- eventually x86 P u
}
\noindent Here {A'} is obtained from {A} by dropping the assumption describing
the loaded code at the old state and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation
ending at the old state, and adding: a code-loading assumption for {u}; the
component-reading assumptions that the instruction does not affect, restated at
{u}; a {MAYCHANGE} relation reaching {u} with the newly-written components
appended; and new assumptions giving the values of the components written by
the instruction (registers, flags, memory, event trace and the instruction
pointer), some of which may introduce fresh variables {z1 ... zp} and extra
derived equations. Assumptions not about machine state are left untouched.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour on
{eventually} goals carrying a symbolic-execution context.

\ENDDOC
